<h3>4. Consider Rounded Mirrors for Softer Interiors</h3>
<img class="alignnone wp-image-10533 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Elegant-modern-spa-small-bathroom-interior.webp" alt="" width="1536" height="1024" />

Bathrooms often contain straight lines and sharp corners.

A round or softly curved mirror introduces balance, helping the space feel more relaxed and contemporary. This works particularly well alongside floating vanities, textured wall tiles and warm timber finishes.

Round mirrors also become a subtle focal point without overwhelming smaller bathrooms, adding soft architectural curves to modern suites as discussed in our guide on <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/open-plan-bathroom-renovation/">open plan bathroom renovation</a>.
<h3>5. Position Mirrors to Reflect Natural Light</h3>
If your bathroom has a window, think carefully about where the mirror is placed.

Positioning it opposite or adjacent to natural light allows daylight to bounce around the room, making the entire space feel brighter.

Even bathrooms with limited daylight can benefit from carefully positioned mirrors that reflect lighter walls, decorative finishes or feature lighting instead of darker corners—a technique frequently specified for <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/knightsbridge/">Knightsbridge luxury residential bathrooms</a>.
<h3>6. Frame the Mirror to Match the Overall Design</h3>
